WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.103
Plumb: an agent harness for inverse physics of dynamic scenes.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:10.514
If we can rebuild a real scene in simulation, physically correct, we can tell whether the top will stop.

3
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:15.676
Here, a coding agent watches a Newton's cradle and refines a model to match it.

4
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.810
Its first passive model is off by sixty-five pixels.

5
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.947
Sweeping the contact stiffness cuts that error by more than half.

6
00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.834
Then it measures the string anchors, and fits seven parameters at once.

7
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.965
The refined simulation has seventy-three percent less error.

8
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.527
Next, a ball rolls into a pyramid of fifteen cups.

9
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:42.426
Rebuilt in the simulator, the same moment can be slowed down, paused, and measured.

10
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.258
The ball strikes at zero point seven three meters per second.

11
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:51.083
Its momentum passes into the cups, and the pyramid comes down.

12
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.528
The agent estimates every parameter, from masses to friction.

13
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:05.987
Outdoors, from a single shot, it recovers the release velocity: seven point eight nine meters per second.

14
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:10.513
The simulation carries the ball along its arc, under gravity, to the rim.

15
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.769
There, its speed, restitution and mass decide how it bounces.

16
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:21.911
From the original camera, the simulated shot lines up with the real one.

17
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:27.229
So, will the top stop? The rebuilt simulation says it will.

18
00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:29.401
Thanks for watching.
